(
 'This package offers the function `Process` which is useful to handle application errors on'
 'a general (or application) level.'
 '`Process` in turn is triggered via `⎕TRAP`. Use the function `HandleError.SetTrap` in order'
 'to set `⎕TRAP` accordingly.'
 ''
 'Note that the main function, `HandleError.Process`, will run in the same thread the error'
 'did occur in. However, while the code of that function is executed, thread switches may well occur.'
 'To prevent other threads from invoking `HandleError.Process` while it is running it uses `:Hold`.'
 ''
 '## Goals'
 ''
 'The goal of `HandleError` is to collect as many pieces of information as possible and make'
 'them accessible so that they can later be used to analyze (and hopefully solve) the problem.\\'
 'In order to achieve this `HandleError` takes the following actions, if technically possible:'
 ''
 '* Create an HTML page with essential information regarding the error, for example:'
 ''
 '  * Error message'
 '  * Stack'
 '  * System vars like `⎕WSID`, `⎕IO`, `⎕ML`, `⎕TNUMS`, `⎕WA`'
 '  * Current directory'
 '  * Command line'
 '  * and more'
 ''
 '* Create a namespace `crash` and populate it with information about the error and, in a'
 '  sub-namespace "Vars", with the variables of the namespace the failing function was running in:'
 '  variables only, no references. `crash` is saved in a component file.'
 ''
 '  In case of a threaded application this and the HTML page might be all you get'
 '  for analyzing the problem.\\'
 ''
 '* Attempt to save an error workspace. Since no workspace can be saved when threads'
 '  are running, `HandleError` kills all threads but the main thread and the one that deals with'
 '  the error. It does so early, before anything is written. That means that even in a multi-threaded'
 '  application you _might_ still get a saved workspace for analyzing purposes if the error happened to'
 '  occur in the main thread.'
 ''
 '  However, since version 4.1 it is possible to prevent the killing of threads by setting'
 '  `killThreads` to 0, meaning it defaults to 1, so no change in behaviour by default.'
 ''
 '  Set `killThreads` to 0 if you intend to let the application continue, for example because an error'
 '  occurred just in a single thread due to invalid input, but the application as such is'
 '  expected to be okay. Typical example for this is a web server that was brought down by'
 '  an ill-formed HTTP request. See "Carrying on after an error" below.'
 ''
 '  Since version 5.1.0 threads are not killed in development unless `enforceOff` is 1, just'
 '  as `HandleError` does not `⎕OFF` there: otherwise the session''s own threads (Link, Cider,'
 '  Tatin...) would be killed as well.'
 ''
 '## The crash files'
 ''
 'All files go into the folder `errorFolder`. They share one name, the name of the workspace followed'
 'by a timestamp in local time that includes milliseconds, for example `MyApp_20261124081809080`:'
 ''
 '* `.html` is the HTML page'
 '* `.dcf` is a component file; its first component holds the namespace `crash`'
 '* `.dws` is the error workspace'
 ''
 'Which of them are written depends on the parameters `createHTML`, `saveCrash` and `saveErrorWS`.'
 'With `stormGuard` 1 the folder also holds the file `StormGuard.txt`.'
 ''
 '## Parameters'
 ''
 'All parameters can be changed by setting variables in a namespace returned by `CreateParms`,'
 'which can then be passed as right argument to the main function `Process`.'
 'For defaults just pass an empty vector to `Process`.'
 'The function `CreateParms` documents all of them.'
 ''
 '## Custom stuff'
 ''
 'You can make `Process` execute your own code by specifying a custom function:'
 ''
 '* `logFunction` is supposed to be the name of a monadic function that returns a shy result.'
 '  That function must be able to deal with a simple character vector as well as vectors of'
 '  character vectors. As the name suggests it will be called by `Process` when something needs'
 '  to be logged.'
 ''
 '* `customFns` can be used to do anything you like. A typical application for this is sending'
 '  an email to notify certain people about the crash. Another possible usage is to add'
 '  something meaningful to `addToMsg` --- that would make it into the HTML report since'
 '  version 5.1.0. You may assign a string or a vector of strings but no HTML.\\'
 ''
 '  `customFns` must accept a right argument. This is the parameter namespace which holds'
 '  all the parameters plus some additional variables:'
 ''
 '  * `crashFilename` holds the name used for the crash files (HTML file, workspace,'
 '    DCF file) but without `.extension`; `∆ERROR_WS` holds the same'
 '  * `LastError` holds `⎕DMX.DM` from the moment of the crash'
 '  * `LastErrorNumber` holds `⎕DMX.EN` at the moment of the crash'
 '  * `EN`, `ENX`, `EM` and `Message` hold what `⎕DMX` had under these names at the moment of the crash'
 '  * `problems` holds a list of texts: what `HandleError` itself ran into while dealing with the crash,'
 '    a log function that cannot be found for example. The HTML page shows them as well.'
 '  * `stormSignature` and `stormCount`, but only with `stormGuard` 1: see "Crash storm guard"\\'
 ''
 '  These copies are necessary because something might go wrong inside `HandleError` and,'
 '  though trapped, as a side effect overwrite `⎕DMX`.\\'
 '  A result of `customFns` is ignored.\\'
 ''
 'The names of both functions must be fully qualified, as in `#.MyApp.Log`. That does not help in case'
 'a function lives in an instance of a class. In such a case specify just the name of the function,'
 'and assign the instance to `logFunctionParent` or `customFnsParent` respectively.\\'
 ''
 '## Carrying on after an error'
 ''
 'What `Process` does once the crash is recorded depends on three parameters, in this order:'
 ''
 '1. If `signal` is not 0, `Process` signals that number. `off` is ignored then.'
 '2. Otherwise, if `off` is 1, it executes `⎕OFF` with `returnCode`, but only in a runtime or with'
 '   `enforceOff` 1.'
 '3. Otherwise it just returns. In a development session it prints a message to the session before'
 '   that. The function that failed does not continue: it is left suspended.'
 ''
 'For an application that is supposed to carry on, a server for example, use `signal`, and make sure'
 'that the application catches that number before `Process` does. `Process` signals it on the very line'
 'that failed, and the trap that called `Process` is still in force there. Being a trap for 0 it catches'
 'the signal as well, so without a precaution `Process` would be called again and again.'
 'Any of the following prevents that; 501 is the number assigned to `signal` here.'
 ''
 'Put a trap for the number in front of what `SetTrap` returns, in the function to restart from:'
 ''
 '~~~'
 '⎕TRAP←(⊂501 ''C'' ''→Restart''),HandleError.SetTrap ''#.ErrorParms'''
 '~~~'
 ''
 'Or put `501 ''N''` in front; the signal then travels on to a `:Trap 501` further up the stack:'
 ''
 '~~~'
 '⎕TRAP←(⊂501 ''N''),HandleError.SetTrap ''#.ErrorParms'''
 '~~~'
 ''
 'Or have a `:Trap 501` between the function that sets `⎕TRAP` and the code that may fail.'
 ''
 'Use a number between 500 and 999 for `signal`.'
 ''
 '## Windows Event Log'
 ''
 '`HandleError` will attempt to write to the Windows Event Log when `windowsEventSource`'
 'is not empty. For that to work .NET and the Dyalog bridge DLLs are required, and the event source'
 'must exist: creating one requires admin rights, which an application usually does not have.'
 'For a runtime application that means shipping the bridge DLLs and creating the source when the'
 'application is installed. In development the DLLs are available anyway.'
 ''
 'When writing the event fails, the HTML page says so under "Problems".'
 ''
 '## Crash storm guard'
 ''
 'When the same error occurs again and again, `Process` writes a full set of crash files and runs the'
 'custom function every time. Setting `stormGuard` to 1 prevents that: an error gets the full treatment'
 'only at its 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th... occurrence, and no more than `stormCeiling` crashes get it within'
 'one hour.'
 'An error that keeps occurring gets the full treatment again once its last one is `stormRepeatAfter`'
 'hours old, whatever its count: an error that occurs once an hour is still reported once a day.'
 ''
 'All other crashes are still written to the log file and the Windows Event Log, with a note saying'
 'how often the error has occurred. No crash files are written for them and the custom function is not'
 'called. Everything else happens as usual: threads are killed, and `Process` ends with `signal` or `⎕OFF`.'
 'An error that has not occurred for `stormResetAfter` hours restarts at 1.'
 ''
 'An error is identified by its error number and the place where it occurred, for example `11 Foo[6]`.'
 'The custom function finds it in `stormSignature`, and the number of occurrences in `stormCount`.'
 ''
 'The counts are kept in the file `StormGuard.txt` in the error folder, so they survive a restart.'
 'All times in that file are UTC.'
 'Therefore only one process may write crash files to a given error folder: `:Hold` serialises crashes'
 'within one process, but not across processes.'
 ''
 'Should the guard fail itself, because it cannot read or write its file for example, the crash gets'
 'the full treatment and the note says why.'
 ''
 '## How tos'
 ''
 '* At an early stage (when we don''t know yet where to save stuff etc) specify an empty vector as'
 '  right argument to `SetTrap`.'
 '* As soon as you can create a parameter space by calling `CreateParms` carry out the following steps:'
 '  1. Execute `#.⎕SHADOW ''ErrorParms''` from within your `⎕LX` function --- note the `#.` in front of'
 '     `⎕SHADOW`; this ensures that it behaves like a normal namespace in root, yet it will disappear'
 '     once your `⎕LX` function quits.'
 ''
 '     That way `Process` will be able to see `#.ErrorParms` from everywhere no matter what.'
 '  2. Create a parameter space like `#.ErrorParms` by calling `HandleError.CreateParms`.'
 '  3. Make amendments'
 '  4. Execute `⎕TRAP←#.HandleError.SetTrap ''#.ErrorParms''`. `SetTrap` requires the fully qualified'
 '     name of the parameter space; it does not accept a reference.'
 ''
 '## Notes'
 ''
 '`HandleError` saves crash files in a folder `Errors` in the current directory on Linux and macOS, while'
 'on Windows a folder with the name of either the EXE or the WS is created within %LOCALAPPDATA%,'
 'with a sub-folder "Errors" inside it. In a clear workspace that name is "ERROR_WS".'
 ''
 'This is because under Windows the application is unlikely to have the right to'
 'write to the directory the EXE was loaded from.\\'
 ''
 'Kai Jaeger'
 ''
 'Homepage: <https://github.com/aplteam/HandleError>'
 ''
 ''
)
